1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,985
Type a tiny angle into a
calculator, in radians.

2
00:00:03,985 --> 00:00:05,866
Say zero point zero one.

3
00:00:05,866 --> 00:00:07,134
Now press sine.

4
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:10,987
You get zero point zero zero
nine nine nine nine eight.

5
00:00:10,987 --> 00:00:13,459
Almost exactly the number you typed in.

6
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:17,039
Aarav spotted this while checking
his trigonometry homework.

7
00:00:17,389 --> 00:00:20,886
For tiny angles, sine x
and x are nearly equal.

8
00:00:20,886 --> 00:00:23,578
So sine x over x gets close to one.

9
00:00:23,578 --> 00:00:24,478
But why?

10
00:00:24,894 --> 00:00:28,112
Today we find out, with limits
of trigonometric functions.

11
00:00:28,762 --> 00:00:30,011
First, a picture.

12
00:00:30,361 --> 00:00:32,576
Draw a circle of radius one.

13
00:00:32,576 --> 00:00:36,433
Mark an angle x at the
centre, measured in radians.

14
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:39,485
The arc this angle cuts off has length x.

15
00:00:39,485 --> 00:00:41,362
That is what a radian means.

16
00:00:41,362 --> 00:00:44,696
And the height of the point
on the circle is sine x.

17
00:00:45,346 --> 00:00:46,706
Now shrink the angle.

18
00:00:46,706 --> 00:00:48,974
Watch the arc and the height together.

19
00:00:49,374 --> 00:00:53,082
At x equals one, sine x
is zero point eight four.

20
00:00:53,082 --> 00:00:57,229
So the ratio, sine x over
x, is zero point eight four.

21
00:00:57,629 --> 00:01:01,984
At x equals zero point five, the
ratio is zero point nine six.

22
00:01:01,984 --> 00:01:05,700
At zero point one, it is
zero point nine nine eight.

23
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,392
The height and the arc
almost become the same line.

24
00:01:09,392 --> 00:01:11,387
Their ratio closes in on one.

25
00:01:12,037 --> 00:01:14,034
But a picture is not a proof.

26
00:01:14,034 --> 00:01:16,060
NCERT uses the sandwich theorem.

27
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:21,439
If g is always between f and h, and
f and h close in on the same number,

28
00:01:21,439 --> 00:01:22,842
then g is trapped.

29
00:01:22,842 --> 00:01:25,398
It must close in on that number too.

30
00:01:25,798 --> 00:01:28,347
Think of cheese between
two slices of bread.

31
00:01:28,347 --> 00:01:31,609
Squeeze the bread together,
and the cheese goes with it.

32
00:01:32,259 --> 00:01:36,844
Here is the proof in small steps,
for x between zero and a right angle.

33
00:01:37,194 --> 00:01:38,094
Step one.

34
00:01:38,009 --> 00:01:42,805
Compare three shapes: a small triangle,
the sector, and a big triangle.

35
00:01:42,805 --> 00:01:44,904
Each one fits inside the next.

36
00:01:45,654 --> 00:01:46,554
Step two.

37
00:01:46,516 --> 00:01:51,669
With radius one, their areas are
half sine x, half x, and half tan x.

38
00:01:51,669 --> 00:01:55,540
So sine x is less than x,
which is less than tan x.

39
00:01:56,290 --> 00:01:57,287
Step three.

40
00:01:57,287 --> 00:02:01,030
Divide by sine x, then
flip each part upside down.

41
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:02,592
The signs flip too.

42
00:02:02,592 --> 00:02:06,800
We get cos x, less than sine
x over x, less than one.

43
00:02:07,550 --> 00:02:08,497
Step four.

44
00:02:08,497 --> 00:02:11,653
As x tends to zero, cos x tends to one.

45
00:02:11,653 --> 00:02:16,376
So sine x over x is squeezed
between two things that tend to one.

46
00:02:16,376 --> 00:02:17,834
Its limit is one.

47
00:02:18,484 --> 00:02:19,668
Side by side.

48
00:02:19,668 --> 00:02:23,294
With x in radians, sine
x over x tends to one.

49
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:25,989
With x in degrees, it does not.

50
00:02:25,989 --> 00:02:30,589
Sine of one degree is about zero point
zero one seven five, so the ratio

51
00:02:30,589 --> 00:02:33,032
tends to pi over one eighty instead.

52
00:02:33,632 --> 00:02:34,603
Now you try.

53
00:02:34,603 --> 00:02:38,027
Does sine x over x tend to
one when x is in degrees?

54
00:02:38,027 --> 00:02:39,235
Pause and decide.

55
00:02:42,435 --> 00:02:43,335
No!

56
00:02:42,848 --> 00:02:43,955
Only in radians.

57
00:02:43,955 --> 00:02:46,689
That is why NCERT measures
x in radians here.

58
00:02:47,339 --> 00:02:49,564
NCERT gives a second result.

59
00:02:49,564 --> 00:02:55,053
The limit of one minus cos x, over
x, as x tends to zero, is zero.

60
00:02:55,453 --> 00:02:56,353
Why?

61
00:02:56,005 --> 00:02:59,981
One minus cos x equals two
sine squared of x over two.

62
00:02:59,981 --> 00:03:05,353
That splits into sine of x over two,
over x over two, which tends to one,

63
00:03:05,353 --> 00:03:08,874
times sine of x over
two, which tends to zero.

64
00:03:08,874 --> 00:03:10,715
One times zero is zero.

65
00:03:11,365 --> 00:03:12,518
Remember this.

66
00:03:12,518 --> 00:03:16,528
In radians, for tiny x,
sine x over x tends to one.

67
00:03:17,228 --> 00:03:18,918
Now, one worked example.

68
00:03:19,268 --> 00:03:24,430
Find the limit of sine five x, over
sine two x, as x tends to zero.

69
00:03:24,930 --> 00:03:25,877
Step one.

70
00:03:25,877 --> 00:03:27,957
Build the pattern on top.

71
00:03:27,957 --> 00:03:32,498
Sine five x is, sine five x
over five x, times five x.

72
00:03:33,248 --> 00:03:34,148
Step two.

73
00:03:34,111 --> 00:03:36,120
Do the same at the bottom.

74
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:40,044
Sine two x is, sine two x
over two x, times two x.

75
00:03:40,794 --> 00:03:41,694
Step three.

76
00:03:41,685 --> 00:03:46,285
Both patterns tend to one, because
five x and two x both tend to zero.

77
00:03:47,035 --> 00:03:47,935
Step four.

78
00:03:47,911 --> 00:03:52,034
What is left is five x over
two x, which is five over two.

79
00:03:52,684 --> 00:03:54,145
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:03:54,495 --> 00:03:57,216
Find the limit of sine three x, over x.

81
00:03:57,216 --> 00:04:00,225
Many students see sine
over x, and write one.

82
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:03,328
But the angle and the bottom must match.

83
00:04:03,328 --> 00:04:06,853
Write it as three, times
sine three x over three x.

84
00:04:06,853 --> 00:04:10,011
That tends to three times
one, which is three.

85
00:04:10,661 --> 00:04:12,161
Where is this asked?

86
00:04:12,161 --> 00:04:17,002
In JEE Main, as in March twenty
twenty one: a tan over a sine, both of

87
00:04:17,002 --> 00:04:19,589
tiny angles, as theta tends to zero.

88
00:04:20,189 --> 00:04:21,376
Exam level now.

89
00:04:21,376 --> 00:04:22,613
Find this limit.

90
00:04:22,613 --> 00:04:23,851
Pause and solve.

91
00:04:29,251 --> 00:04:30,644
The answer is A.

92
00:04:30,644 --> 00:04:31,638
Two thirds.

93
00:04:32,138 --> 00:04:35,897
Use one minus cos two x
equals two sine squared x.

94
00:04:36,397 --> 00:04:38,122
Rearrange into patterns.

95
00:04:38,122 --> 00:04:43,416
Two, times sine x over x, squared,
times three x over sine three x, times

96
00:04:43,416 --> 00:04:44,337
one third.

97
00:04:44,837 --> 00:04:46,693
Each pattern tends to one.

98
00:04:46,693 --> 00:04:50,625
So the limit is two times one
third, which is two thirds.

99
00:04:51,225 --> 00:04:52,765
Here is your recap.

100
00:04:52,765 --> 00:04:54,348
Screenshot this card.

101
00:04:57,548 --> 00:04:59,734
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

102
00:05:00,134 --> 00:05:01,034
One.

103
00:05:00,638 --> 00:05:04,640
Why must x be in radians for
sine x over x to tend to one?

104
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:06,440
Two.

105
00:05:06,055 --> 00:05:10,552
What is the limit of sine seven
x, over x, as x tends to zero?

106
00:05:11,452 --> 00:05:12,352
Three.

107
00:05:12,052 --> 00:05:15,764
Which theorem traps sine x
over x between cos x and one?

108
00:05:16,864 --> 00:05:20,050
Next time: a speedometer shows
your speed at one instant.

109
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:22,608
How can there be a speed
at a single moment?

110
00:05:22,608 --> 00:05:25,534
We find out with the derivative
from first principles.
